WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.304
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:09.304
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:16.610
Unit seventeen: Existentialism. Today you will reason about meaning using subjunctive-style hypotheticals.

4
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.701
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.865
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:26.078
Existentialism.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:32.194
Existentialism means the philosophy that individuals create their own meaning through free choice.

8
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:36.548
Existentialism places responsibility for meaning on the individual.

9
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.011
Your turn. Say: Existentialism.

10
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.321
Existentialism.

11
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:46.270
Authenticity.

12
00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:52.023
Authenticity means living in accordance with one's own values rather than social pressure.

13
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:56.369
Authenticity demands honesty about what one truly values.

14
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.868
Your turn. Say: Authenticity.

15
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:03.851
Authenticity.

16
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:05.641
Absurdism.

17
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.610
Absurdism means the view that humans seek meaning in a universe that offers none.

18
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:16.225
Camus's absurdism rejects both despair and false comfort.

19
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:19.748
Your turn. Say: Absurdism.

20
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.740
Absurdism.

21
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:25.810
Radical freedom.

22
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:32.241
Radical freedom means Sartre's idea that humans are free and wholly responsible for their choices.

23
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.349
Radical freedom removes every excuse.

24
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.660
Your turn. Say: Radical freedom.

25
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.849
Radical freedom.

26
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:44.666
Bad faith.

27
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.565
Bad faith means self-deception in which one denies one's own freedom.

28
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.264
Blaming circumstances for every choice is bad faith.

29
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.804
Your turn. Say: Bad faith.

30
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:00.923
Bad faith.

31
00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:02.568
Angst.

32
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:07.998
Angst means deep anxiety arising from the awareness of freedom and mortality.

33
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:10.525
Angst is the dizziness of freedom.

34
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:13.432
Your turn. Say: Angst.

35
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.210
Angst.

36
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.497
Self-determination.

37
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:24.248
Self-determination means the power to decide one's own course of life.

38
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.674
Self-determination is both a gift and a burden.

39
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:31.307
Your turn. Say: Self-determination.

40
00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:35.725
Self-determination.

41
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:37.682
Meaning-making.

42
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:42.599
Meaning-making means the active process of creating purpose in one's life.

43
00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:46.472
Meaning-making can happen through work, love and commitment.

44
00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:49.538
Your turn. Say: Meaning-making.

45
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.144
Meaning-making.

46
00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:58.329
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:03.458
Visal, if life has no built-in purpose, why bother doing anything?

48
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:11.080
That is the existentialist's starting point. Were meaning not given, we would need to create it ourselves.

49
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:13.916
That sounds exhausting.

50
00:03:15.017 --> 00:03:22.731
It can be. Had we been handed a script, we would have been spared the angst, but also the authorship of our own lives.

51
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:25.329
So freedom is the burden?

52
00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:35.071
And the gift. Should we refuse the responsibility, we would be living in bad faith, pretending that circumstances choose for us.

53
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:37.641
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:41.864
Grammar focus: Subjunctive-style hypotheticals.

55
00:03:42.164 --> 00:03:43.363
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.060
Were meaning not given, we would need to create it.

57
00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:51.494
Had we been handed a script, we would have been spared the angst.

58
00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:55.834
Should we refuse responsibility, we would be living in bad faith.

59
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:06.662
Inversion replaces 'if': Were X (= If X were), Had X (= If X had), Should X (= If X should). Negative: Were meaning not given (not 'Weren't meaning given').

60
00:04:07.417 --> 00:04:12.043
Be careful! Don't say: If meaning would not be given, we create it.

61
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.346
Say: Were meaning not given, we would create it.

62
00:04:17.646 --> 00:04:20.145
Inversion + would for hypotheticals.

63
00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:23.644
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.433 --> 00:04:25.852
Condemned to Be Free.

65
00:04:26.152 --> 00:04:30.799
Existentialism begins from the claim that human beings are not born with a fixed purpose.

66
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:39.895
Sartre argued that existence precedes essence: we exist first and then define ourselves through our choices, a condition he called radical freedom.

67
00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.499
Were meaning handed to us, we would be relieved of responsibility, but we would also lose authorship of our lives.

68
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:54.759
Camus, writing about absurdism, accepted that the universe is silent, yet insisted that one must imagine Sisyphus happy.

69
00:04:55.767 --> 00:05:03.060
Authenticity, for existentialists, means choosing deliberately instead of hiding in bad faith behind roles and excuses.

70
00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:08.122
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.983 --> 00:05:11.424
blank meaning not given, we would need to create it.

72
00:05:11.724 --> 00:05:14.576
A, Was. B, Were. C, Is.

73
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:19.715
The answer is B: Were.

74
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.720
Self-deception that denies one's freedom is blank.

75
00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.767
A, absurdism. B, bad faith. C, angst.

76
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.392
The answer is B: bad faith.

77
00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.805
The deep anxiety of freedom is blank.

78
00:05:37.105 --> 00:05:41.130
A, angst. B, authenticity. C, meaning-making.

79
00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:46.515
The answer is A: angst.

80
00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.359
Choose the correct inversion.

81
00:05:49.659 --> 00:05:55.542
A, Had we known, we would have acted.. B, Had we knew, we would acted.. C, Have we known, we would act..

82
00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.237
The answer is A: Had we known, we would have acted..

83
00:06:03.383 --> 00:06:05.998
According to the reading, authenticity means…

84
00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:11.960
A, hiding behind roles. B, choosing deliberately. C, avoiding choices.

85
00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.092
The answer is B: choosing deliberately.

86
00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.277
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.117 --> 00:06:27.160
How might an existentialist respond to the feeling that life lacks inherent meaning?

88
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.439
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.739 --> 00:07:12.072
An existentialist would say that the absence of given meaning is not a tragedy but a task. Were meaning handed to us, we would lose the freedom to author our lives. We must therefore create it through commitments, relationships and work. This is demanding, since no one can choose on our behalf, but it is also dignifying. Meaning is something we make rather than find.

90
00:07:12.372 --> 00:07:14.325
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.217 --> 00:07:20.005
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and subjunctive-style hypotheticals.

92
00:07:20.305 --> 00:07:24.978
Today's value is authentic living. Live by choices that are truly your own.

93
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:27.965
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:28.265 --> 00:07:30.813
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

